      "id": 1,
      "text": "Liberty was settled around 1797 by Adam Dale, a Revolutionary War veteran who built a mill on Smith Fork Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberty%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Liberty’s Main Street was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1987 as the Liberty Historic District.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberty%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The district showcases Greek Revival, Gothic Revival, Queen Anne, Classical Revival, and Craftsman styles in its one‑to‑two‑story frame buildings.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/liberty_historic_district_liberty_tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Allen Bluff Mule, a limestone bluff painting, is a local icon, though residents debate whether Lavader Woodard or a stock‑farm ad painted it.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberty%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "After Dale’s arrival, about forty families moved to Liberty by 1804, expanding the mill’s surrounding homesteads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/liberty_historic_district_liberty_tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Early development of Liberty followed Native American trails and land grants, shaping its initial paths before DeKalb County split from Smith County in 1837.",
